      text: "After fifteen years of supervising postgraduate students, I have read thousands of essays, and I can say with some confidence that the problems I encounter are rarely about intelligence. Most of my students are perfectly capable of original thought. What lets them down, time and again, is the way their ideas are presented. Below are the mistakes I see most often, along with some suggestions for avoiding them. The first, and by far the most common, is the absence of a clear thesis statement. An essay is not a collection of interesting facts; it is an argument. If a reader cannot summarise your central claim in a single sentence after reading the introduction, something has gone wrong. Everything that follows should lend weight to that claim, and any paragraph that does not should be cut, however much effort went into it. The second problem is almost the opposite of the first: making assertions without evidence. Phrases such as 'everyone knows' or 'it is obvious that' should set alarm bells ringing. If something is genuinely obvious, it does not need saying; if it is not, it needs a source. Equally dangerous is the tendency to overgeneralise from a single study. One experiment, however striking, rarely warrants such confident claims, and it is risky to extrapolate from it. Good academic writers are careful to add caveats and to signal how confident they are, using tentative language like 'the data suggest' rather than 'this proves'. Third, many students ignore counterarguments or play devil's advocate only half-heartedly, presumably because they fear that acknowledging opposing views will weaken their case. In fact, the reverse is true. Showing that you have considered the strongest objections to your position, and explaining why you remain unconvinced, makes your argument far more persuasive. Fourth, there is style. There is a persistent myth that academic writing should be dense and difficult, and that long words signal intelligence. Nothing could be further from the truth. Verbose sentences crammed with jargon merely make the reader work harder. The best scholarly prose I know is concise and succinct, and it delineates complex ideas in plain language. Finally, a word about sources. Plagiarism is not only copying someone else's text word for word; it also includes paraphrasing their ideas without acknowledgement. When in doubt, cite. Keep careful notes as you read, so that you always know where an idea came from, and leave time at the end to check that every reference in the text appears in your bibliography. None of this is particularly difficult, but all of it requires time. My final piece of advice, therefore, is the simplest: finish your draft early, put it away for a few days, and then proofread it with fresh eyes. Rarely will you regret the extra effort.",
