      text: "When Irina Volkova arrived in Toronto four years ago, she could order a coffee and introduce herself, albeit with a great deal of hesitation. Today she works as a project coordinator for an engineering firm, writes reports in English every week and has recently passed the C1 Advanced exam. We asked her what she had learnt along the way. The first lesson, she says, is that progress is never a straight line. 'The learning curve in the first year was incredibly steep. Every week I could feel myself getting the hang of something new. Then, somewhere around B2, I hit a plateau that lasted almost a year. I was studying just as hard, but nothing seemed to change.' Irina believes this experience is almost universal, and arguably the point at which most adult learners give up. 'What nobody tells you is that the improvements at that stage are real but much less tangible. You are not learning new tenses any more; you are learning precision.' Breaking through the plateau required a change of approach. Irina began to scrutinise her own speech by recording meetings, with her colleagues' permission, and listening back. The results were uncomfortable. 'I discovered a handful of fossilised errors I had been making for years: wrong prepositions, missing articles, the same three linking words over and over. Nobody had ever corrected me, because people could understand me perfectly well.' She made a list of these mistakes and worked on them one at a time, thereby turning a vague sense of frustration into a concrete plan. She is also a firm believer in spaced repetition. 'For years I wrote words in a notebook and never looked at them again, so my retention was terrible. Reviewing words at increasing intervals, just before you forget them, is far more efficient. By and large, it is not how many words you meet that matters, but how many you consolidate.' Not everything she tried worked, and she describes much of her journey as a process of trial and error. Watching films with English subtitles helped, but only when she paused regularly to note down useful phrases. Grammar exercises bolstered her confidence but did little for her fluency on their own. And she warns against perfectionism, which she considers highly detrimental. 'If you are afraid of making mistakes, you simply stop speaking, and that exacerbates the problem.' Her advice to learners preparing for C1? Make regular self-assessment part of your routine, using the official can-do descriptors as a benchmark. Create an environment conducive to learning, even if that just means changing the language settings on your phone. And keep using the language after the exam. 'Skills you don't use get rusty surprisingly quickly. Even now, after a month's holiday in Russia, I need a few days to brush up on my English.' In a nutshell, she says, reaching an advanced level is less about talent than about persistence. 'There is nothing magical about it. What is paramount is to keep going, especially in the months when you can't see any progress at all.'",
