    text: "It is not so long ago that the idea of a machine deciding who should get a job, a loan or parole would have seemed like the stuff of science fiction. Today, algorithmic systems are routinely used to screen job applicants, set insurance premiums and, in some jurisdictions, advise judges on the likelihood that a defendant will reoffend. Their advocates argue that such tools are faster, cheaper and, crucially, more consistent than human beings, who are notoriously prone to fatigue, prejudice and mood. Their critics are considerably less convinced. The case for algorithms rests largely on the well-documented failings of human judgement. Studies of judicial decisions have suggested that rulings can vary depending on factors as arbitrary as the time of day, and recruiters have been shown to favour candidates whose names, accents or backgrounds resemble their own. Seen in this light, a system that applies the same criteria to everyone appears not merely efficient but fair. The difficulty, however, lies in the data on which these systems are trained. An algorithm learns by identifying patterns in past decisions, and if those decisions were themselves biased, the machine will faithfully reproduce, and potentially amplify, that bias. One widely reported recruitment tool, having been trained on a decade of applications submitted mostly by men, reportedly learnt to downgrade CVs containing the word 'women's', as in 'women's chess club'. Had its developers examined its outputs more carefully, the flaw might have been detected far sooner. What makes such cases particularly troubling is not that the system was malicious, but that its bias was hidden behind an appearance of mathematical neutrality. Transparency presents a further challenge. Many of the most powerful systems are, in effect, black boxes: even their designers cannot fully explain why a particular output was produced. This sits uneasily with fundamental legal principles. In most democratic societies, it is considered essential that a person who is refused a benefit or sentenced to prison be told the reasons for that decision and be able to challenge them. A recommendation that cannot be explained can scarcely be contested. None of this is to suggest that algorithms should be abandoned altogether. Used with care, they can and do help to expose inconsistencies that might otherwise go unnoticed. The question, rather, is one of accountability. Several governments are now drafting legislation requiring that high-risk systems be independently audited, that their training data be documented and that a human being retain responsibility for the final decision. Whether such measures will prove effective remains to be seen; technology has a habit of moving faster than the laws designed to regulate it. Perhaps the deepest issue is psychological rather than technical. There is considerable evidence that people tend to defer to automated recommendations, even when their own judgement suggests otherwise, a phenomenon known as automation bias. If that is the case, then keeping 'a human in the loop' offers less protection than it might appear. Ultimately, the challenge is not simply to build fairer machines, but to remain sceptical enough to question them.",
